# Use Brian environment
#
# No environment variable is required when using ChatGPT subscription sign-in.
# API-key backends remain available as alternatives below.
# `pnpm dev` auto-generates JWT_SECRET and defaults to an embedded PGLite
# store under ~/.usebrian/, so you do not need to set anything else here.
# Copy this file to .env if you like, or just let the launcher prompt you
# for a provider on first run. Complete ChatGPT authorization in
# Settings → AI providers after the app opens.

# ── Optional API-key model backends ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The `gemini` provider can run on AI Studio (GEMINI_API_KEY) or Vertex AI
# (VERTEX_PROJECT_ID), and a deployment can run Qwen-only via DASHSCOPE_API_KEY
# below. Pick what your region can reach: Google blocks the AI Studio developer
# API in some regions (Hong Kong, mainland China), where Vertex (asia-east2) or
# DashScope work instead. See docs/architecture/engine/provider-abstraction.md.

# Option A — Google AI Studio. Free key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Option B — Vertex AI (same Gemini models, reachable in blocked regions).
# On GCP (Cloud Run/GCE) no key is needed: credentials come from the metadata
# server (ADC); grant the runtime service account roles/aiplatform.user.
# VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=my-gcp-project
# VERTEX_LOCATION=asia-east2          # host + regional quota pool (default asia-east2)
# VERTEX_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=        # only off-GCP: full service-account key JSON
# Long-recording transcription also requires GCS_FILES_BUCKET below. Vertex has
# no Files API, so temporary audio objects are uploaded there, referenced by
# gs:// URI, then deleted. Grant the runtime identity object create/read/delete.

# Option C — Qwen-only (no Google credential): set DASHSCOPE_API_KEY below.
# PDF/office distillation uses qwen-long file upload on DashScope. PDFs also
# render up to 10 pages locally for Qwen-VL visual analysis.

# ── Voice notes and recordings (on by default) ──────────────────────────────
# Inbound voice notes on messaging channels are transcribed with the media
# backend configured above (Gemini, Vertex, or Qwen-ASR) before the turn runs.
# VOICE_TRANSCRIPTION_ENABLED is an ops kill switch, ON unless set to
# false/0 — with it off, a voice note reaches the assistant with no audio and
# no transcript, and it can only tell the user it received nothing.
# VOICE_TRANSCRIPTION_ENABLED=false
# Model id for short inline voice notes. Unset = gemini-2.5-flash (ignored by
# DashScope, which selects Qwen-ASR).
# VOICE_TRANSCRIPTION_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
#
# LONG recordings (audio/video files, web uploads, channel attachments) run
# through the recording worker, which shells out to `ffprobe` and `ffmpeg`.
# Both must be on PATH or every recording fails with "ffprobe prerequisite
# failed". Install with: apt-get install -y ffmpeg   (macOS: brew install ffmpeg)

# ── PDF output (headless LibreOffice) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Every PDF Brian produces (Office Document/Presentation/Spreadsheet releases,
# the doc page "Export as PDF" download, and the `renderPdf` chat tool) is
# rendered by `soffice --headless --convert-to pdf`. Install with:
#   apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends libreoffice-writer-nogui \
#     libreoffice-calc-nogui libreoffice-impress-nogui fonts-liberation fonts-noto-cjk
#   (macOS: brew install --cask libreoffice)
# Without it those paths fail honestly with "PDF rendering is unavailable".
# The binary is auto-detected (/usr/bin/soffice, the macOS app bundle, PATH);
# set LIBREOFFICE_BIN only for a non-standard install. At most
# LIBREOFFICE_MAX_CONCURRENCY conversions run at once per process (default 2 -
# a headless soffice peaks at a few hundred MB).
# LIBREOFFICE_BIN=/usr/bin/soffice
# LIBREOFFICE_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2

# ── Web search (optional - falls back to DuckDuckGo when unset) ──────────────
# Set any one of these to upgrade the web-search tool beyond the free
# DuckDuckGo default. The first configured provider wins.
TAVILY_API_KEY=          # Tavily search (AI-optimized results)
BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=    # Brave Search API
SERPER_API_KEY=          # Serper (Google Search results)
JINA_API_KEY=            # Jina Reader for cleaner page fetches (works without a key at lower rate limits)

# ── Google Maps location intelligence (optional) ────────────────────────────
# Dedicated server key restricted to Maps Grounding Lite API. When set, Brain
# gains place-search, weather, and walking/driving route tools. Do not reuse a
# browser-visible Google Picker key.
GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_API_KEY=

# ── X / Twitter reading (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# urlReader reads x.com / twitter.com status permalinks through the official
# X API v2 tweet lookup when TWITTER_BEARER_TOKEN is set (app-only Bearer from
# a Basic-tier+ X developer app; the Free tier cannot read tweets), and falls
# back to xAI Grok otherwise. XAI_API_KEY also enables the xSearch tool, which
# queries X via Grok, and reads non-permalink X URLs (profiles, search).
TWITTER_BEARER_TOKEN=
XAI_API_KEY=

# ── Model fallback (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# An optional resilience fallback used if Gemini is unavailable.
# Set FALLBACK_PROVIDER_ENABLED=true and provide an Anthropic key.
FALLBACK_PROVIDER_ENABLED=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# AES-256-GCM key for bearer credentials on workspace custom OpenAI-compatible
# endpoints. No-auth local endpoints work without it. The value must decode to
# exactly 32 bytes. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32. Never rotate it after
# saving credentials, or the stored credentials can no longer be decrypted.
LLM_PROVIDER_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# OpenAI-compatible endpoint key (DashScope — Qwen / DeepSeek). Leave empty to
# run Gemini-only. Set it (with no GEMINI_API_KEY / VERTEX_PROJECT_ID) to run
# QWEN-ONLY: chat then defaults to a Qwen model automatically — the gemini
# provider is not registered, so its models are absent from routing and menus.
DASHSCOPE_API_KEY=
# DashScope host override. Unset = international (Singapore). Set to the Beijing
# endpoint for a mainland-China deployment. Applies to Qwen chat, embeddings,
# and media (distill/voice).
# DASHSCOPE_BASE_URL=https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1
# DashScope media-model id overrides. The built-in defaults (qwen-vl-max for
# images/PDF pages, qwen3-asr-flash for audio, qwen-long for PDF/office text) are
# not guaranteed to exist on every Model Studio endpoint/region — a mismatch
# surfaces as a "model not found" error during image/audio/PDF understanding.
# Set to a model id your DashScope account actually serves.
# DASHSCOPE_VISION_MODEL=qwen-vl-max
# DASHSCOPE_ASR_MODEL=qwen3-asr-flash
# DASHSCOPE_LONG_MODEL=qwen-long
# Long-recording async transcription model (separate from short voice ASR).
# DASHSCOPE_FILETRANS_MODEL=qwen3-asr-flash-filetrans
# Model for the watched browser-use exploration (browserExplore). Optional —
# defaults per available key: Anthropic => Haiku, else Gemini => Flash.
BROWSER_USE_MODEL=

# ── My Browser and cloud browser use (optional) ─────────────────────────────
# `pnpm start` launches the local browser relay on the first available loopback
# port beginning at 8094. It generates and persists the shared relay secret in
# ~/.usebrian/config.json and passes the selected URL and secret to the API.
# Set BOTH values only when using a separately managed relay; an explicit URL
# prevents the launcher from starting its local relay.
BROWSER_RELAY_URL=
BROWSER_RELAY_SECRET=
# Saved browser-session encryption key (base64 of exactly 32 bytes). The
# launcher generates and persists this in ~/.usebrian/config.json when unset.
# For API-only deployments, generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
BROWSER_VAULT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Separate encryption key for saved browser usernames/passwords. The launcher
# generates and persists it when unset. API-only deployments: openssl rand -base64 32
BROWSER_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# E2B enables the cloud browser backend. Both values come from your E2B account
# and template build; leave them unset to use only the local browser extension.
# Build instructions: docs/plans/e2b-template-setup.md
E2B_API_KEY=
E2B_TEMPLATE_ID=
# To side-load the Chromium extension, run:
#   pnpm --filter @use-brian/browser-extension build
# Then open chrome://extensions (or edge://extensions), enable Developer mode,
# choose "Load unpacked", and select apps/browser-extension/dist. Pair it from
# Settings -> Browser profiles after Use Brian is running.

# ── Advanced / self-hosting overrides (optional) ────────────────────────────
# Point at an external Postgres connection string to use it instead of the
# embedded PGLite brain. Must be PostgreSQL 18 (the baseline schema sets
# transaction_timeout, which PG<=17 rejects) with the vector + pg_trgm
# extensions installed. Unset -> the launcher runs the embedded PGLite brain
# on :54329. When set, the launcher does NOT auto-migrate: run
#   pnpm --filter @use-brian/api migrate
# once before `pnpm dev`.
DATABASE_URL=
#
# ── Chat message store (the archive of every channel message) ───────────────
# The launcher automatically discovers a sibling brian-message-store checkout,
# generates/persists the HMAC secret, and binds the service to 127.0.0.1:8092.
# The archive runs against its OWN database, separate from the brain: point
# BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_DATABASE_URL at a database owned by a dedicated
# NOSUPERUSER role (the store refuses to start as a superuser, because a
# superuser bypasses the FORCE row-level security that isolates each owner's
# messages) with the vector + pg_trgm extensions installed. Unset -> the local
# chat archive is disabled for the session (channels still work; nothing is
# recorded for searchChatHistory / backfill).
BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_DATABASE_URL=
# Source-checkout overrides only (normally auto-discovered):
# BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_ENABLED=0
# BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_DIR=/absolute/path/to/brian-message-store
# BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_BIN=/absolute/path/to/brian-message-store
# BRIAN_MESSAGE_STORE_MAX_BODY_BYTES=8388608
# Auto-generated and persisted to ~/.usebrian/config.json if left unset.
JWT_SECRET=
# Shared secret for the API <-> doc-sync internal routes (the auto-on-save brain
# ingest enqueue + AI doc writes). Auto-generated and persisted to
# ~/.usebrian/config.json like JWT_SECRET; only set this by hand if you run the
# api and doc-sync as separate processes WITHOUT the launcher (both need the
# same value).
DOC_SYNC_SECRET=
# AES-GCM key (base64 of 32 bytes) that encrypts connector OAuth refresh-tokens
# and PATs at rest in connector_instance.credentials, and BYO channel bot
# credentials (Telegram/Slack/Discord bot tokens) in
# channel_integrations.credentials. Auto-generated and persisted to
# ~/.usebrian/config.json by the launcher; only set this by hand if you run
# the api WITHOUT the launcher. Without it, connecting a connector or a
# channel returns 503. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
CHANNEL_CREDENTIAL_KEY=
# Optional paid Feed Cloud Link endpoint override. The official
# https://api.usebrian.ai service is the default; set this only for staging or
# another compatible managed service. It enables Threads/X connection,
# publishing, insights, and inspiration while the brain and planning database
# stay self-hosted.
# MANAGED_FEED_CLOUD_URL=https://api.usebrian.ai
# Application-default file storage for self-hosted deployments. When set and
# GCS_FILES_BUCKET is unset, workspace files, avatars, recordings, and channel
# media are stored below this directory. Browser uploads, connector streams,
# public media, and byte-range playback are served through short-lived signed
# API URLs, so they do not require GCS. Mount this directory on durable storage
# in Docker or Kubernetes. If both are set, GCS_FILES_BUCKET takes precedence.
# When both are unset, local development falls back to ephemeral
# /tmp/sidanclaw-files. GCS_FILES_BUCKET is required for Vertex long-recording
# transcription.
# GCS_FILES_BUCKET=my-use-brian-files
# LOCAL_FILES_DIR=/var/lib/use-brian/files
# Optional public HTTPS base for the signed `/api/local-files` endpoint. Set
# this when a remote URL-only processor such as DashScope file transcription
# must fetch locally stored bytes. Keep API_URL on localhost; expose only
# `/api/local-files` through a tunnel/reverse proxy at this origin.
# LOCAL_FILES_PUBLIC_URL=https://files.example.com
# Override the default port the app listens on.
PORT=
# Default wall-clock (ms) for a workflow `assistant_call` step / scheduled job /
# A2A consult that sets no explicit `depth`. Absent → 90000 (90s). Clamped to
# [1000, 300000]; a non-numeric value falls back to the default. An explicit
# step `depth.timeoutMs` or a `deep` tier still wins per-field.
ASSISTANT_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS=

# ── Messaging channels (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Studio → Channels connects your own Telegram / Slack / Discord bots; the
# storage + webhooks are part of the open core (CHANNEL_CREDENTIAL_KEY above is
# the only hard requirement). Two practical notes for self-hosting:
#
#   * Inbound webhooks need a PUBLICLY REACHABLE https base URL. Telegram
#     connect registers `${API_URL}/webhook/telegram/<channelId>` with
#     Telegram, and Slack needs `${API_URL}/webhook/slack/<channelId>` in its
#     app's Event Subscriptions — so when running locally, put a tunnel
#     (e.g. `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:4000`, ngrok, Tailscale
#     Funnel) in front of the api and set API_URL to the tunnel URL before
#     connecting. The launcher defaults API_URL to http://localhost:4000,
#     which Telegram/Slack cannot reach.
API_URL=
#
#   * Discord does not use webhooks — it needs a persistent Gateway WebSocket.
#     `pnpm dev` starts the open `apps/discord-connector` locally on port 8090
#     and generates/persists its shared secret automatically. Set both values
#     below only to use an externally deployed connector instead. The external
#     service needs the same secret as the API and must relay inbound events to
#     `${API_URL}/internal/discord/inbound`.
DISCORD_CONNECTOR_URL=
# Auto-generated under ~/.usebrian/ for local boot. Required on both the API
# and connector when deploying the connector separately.
DISCORD_CONNECTOR_SECRET=
# WhatsApp BYON also uses a persistent bridge. `pnpm dev` starts the open
# connector locally on port 8091, points it at the same database as the API,
# sets its local filesystem credential directory to ~/.usebrian/wa-creds, and
# generates/persists the shared secret. Set both values only to use an external
# connector; that service must use the same DATABASE_URL as the API for BYON
# credential persistence and call back to `${API_URL}`.
WA_CONNECTOR_URL=
WA_CONNECTOR_SECRET=

# WeChat *bot* pairing and inbound polling use the local iLink bridge. `pnpm
# start` launches it on port 8093 and generates/persists the shared secret.
# Set these only when running that bridge separately on this machine. This is
# the sanctioned iLink bot channel (a new bot contact, DMs only, no history).
WECHAT_CONNECTOR_URL=
WECHAT_CONNECTOR_SECRET=

# Feishu/Lark uses the official long-connection SDK. `pnpm start` launches the
# bridge locally on port 8095 and generates/persists this secret. Set both only
# when running the bridge separately.
FEISHU_CONNECTOR_URL=
FEISHU_CONNECTOR_SECRET=
#
# The PERSONAL-account WeChat mirror (the user's own account, groups, history)
# is a different thing: a "custom channel" driven by a bridge you run. It needs
# NO env here -- create the channel in Studio -> Channels -> + Add channel ->
# Custom (kind "wechat-desktop"), copy the one-time ubc_ token, and run
# use-brian/apps/wechat-desktop-bridge (a container runtime is required -- the
# agent-wechat WeChat client is container-only) on a Linux box against
# `${API_URL}/bridge/v1/channels/<id>`. See
# docs/architecture/channels/custom-channel.md + wechat-desktop.md.

# ── Connectors (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Built-in connectors (Google Calendar/Gmail/Drive, Notion, Fathom, Microsoft
# Teams, GitHub) are off until you bring your own OAuth app credentials. GitHub
# uses a Personal Access Token entered in the UI and needs nothing here. The OAuth providers
# resolve their app client id/secret from ~/.usebrian/connectors.config.json
# (override path: CONNECTORS_CONFIG_PATH), e.g.
#   { "google": { "clientId": "...", "clientSecret": "..." },
#     "msgraph": { "clientId": "...", "clientSecret": "..." } }
# or from these env vars as a fallback. Unset → that connector stays unavailable.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
NOTION_CLIENT_ID=
NOTION_CLIENT_SECRET=
FATHOM_CLIENT_ID=
FATHOM_CLIENT_SECRET=
MSGRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
MSGRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── AI Engines MCP (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read-only observation of external AI answer engines,
# served at POST /api/engines/mcp and registered in a workspace as a custom
# MCP connector (URL + the bearer secret below). Dark by default: without
# ENGINES_MCP_SECRET plus at least one credential the route does not exist.
# These are observation-instrument credentials, NOT the model keys that power
# your assistant (GEMINI_API_KEY above is a different job). Spec:
# docs/architecture/integrations/engines-mcp.md in the platform repo.
ENGINES_MCP_SECRET=
ENGINES_OPENAI_API_KEY=
ENGINES_GEMINI_API_KEY=
ENGINES_PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=
ENGINES_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ENGINES_DAILY_CALL_CAP=        # runaway breaker; default 200 upstream calls per UTC day; 0 disables
